WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.173
What the numbers say.

2
00:00:01.453 --> 00:00:11.160
With these assumptions, a warm cache makes the whole-corpus prompt about as cheap per question as a RAG prompt sixteen times shorter: 3 cents against 3.

3
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.720
6 cents of input.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.773
Uncached, it is more than sixteen times the price.

5
00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:18.485
Two conditions make the cached number real.

6
00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:33.869
Traffic must be steady enough that the five-minute cache rarely expires (otherwise you pay 75 cents in writes again and again, or you pay for the one-hour lifetime at twice the input price per write), and the corpus must stay the same between writes.

7
00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:37.627
Now change one assumption: a 2-million-token corpus.

8
00:00:37.907 --> 00:00:44.285
It no longer fits in the context window, and even a cached read would cost 40 cents per question on this model.

9
00:00:44.565 --> 00:00:45.803
Retrieval is back.

10
00:00:46.083 --> 00:01:04.387
Or switch the generator to claude-haiku-5-5: the input price is far lower, but prompts over 100,000 tokens are billed at a higher rate on that model, which is the kind of detail that makes “check the pricing page” a real instruction rather than a disclaimer.

11
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:12.517
Run the numbers for your corpus, then run the eval, because the cheapest configuration that fails your accuracy bar is not cheap.
